				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{array}{cc}{\pi }_{k}=P\left({z}_{1}=k\right), & k\in \left\{1,\ldots ,K\right\}\end{array}$$\end{document}πk=Pz1=k,k∈1,…,Kwhere z indicates the state and K is the total number of states. The probability of state i transitioning to state j is represented by the K × K matrix, T, in which each row sums to 1:2\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{array}{cc}{T}_{i,j}=T\big({z}_{i}\to {z}_{j}\big)=P\big({z}_{j}{{{{{\rm{|}}}}}}{z}_{i}\big), & i,j\in \left\{1,\ldots ,K\right\}\end{array}$$\end{document}Ti,j=Tzi→zj=Pzj∣zi,i,j∈1,…,K

The emission likelihood matrix, EL, is based on the cell observations. It is defined as the probability of an observation conditioned on the cell being in a specific state:3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${EL}\left(n,k\right)=P\left({x}_{n}=x{{{{{\rm{|}}}}}}{z}_{n}=k\right)$$\end{document}ELn,k=Pxn=x∣zn=kwhere xn is the observation for cell number n, with a total of N cells in a lineage. Separate observations were assumed to be independent; for instance, cell fate is assumed to be independent from the duration of each cell phase. This facilitates calculating the likelihood of observations, because we can multiply the likelihood of all observations together for the overall likelihood.

