				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${T}_{i,j}^{* }=\frac{{\sum }_{n=1}^{N-1}{\xi }_{n}(i,j)}{{\sum }_{n=1}^{N-1}{\gamma }_{n}(i)}$$\end{document}Ti,j*=∑n=1N−1ξn(i,j)∑n=1N−1γn(i)where21\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\xi }_{n}(i,j)={\left(\frac{{\gamma }_{p(n)}(i)}{\frac{{\beta }_{n}(i)}{{MSD}(n,i)}T(i,j)}\right)}^{T}\times \frac{{\beta }_{n}(j)}{{MSD}(n,j)}$$\end{document}ξn(i,j)=γp(n)(i)βn(i)MSD(n,i)T(i,j)T×βn(j)MSD(n,j)

The expectation-maximization algorithm consists of two steps: expectation and maximization. During expectation, the probabilities of all cells being in specific states are calculated, such that for every cell and every state we have \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
