To compare the tHMM with a classifier that ignores heritability, we manually calculated the optimal classification boundary between the gamma distributions for state 1 and state 2. The best choice of classification boundary between two gamma distributions is the point at which the likelihood of the random variable, x, is equal between the two distributions:24\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$p\left(x\big|{G}\left({{k}}_{1},{\theta }_{1}\right)\right)=p\left(x\big|{G}\left({{k}}_{2},{\theta }_{2}\right)\right)$$\end{document}pxGk1,θ1=pxGk2,θ2where k1, θ1, k2, and θ2 are the shape and scale parameters of the gamma distribution corresponding to state 1 and 2, respectively. The shape parameter was shared between the two distributions. Consequently, this can be simplified to:25\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$x=\frac{k\,{{{{{\rm{ln}}}}}}\frac{{\theta }_{2}}{{\theta }_{1}}}{\frac{1}{{\theta }_{1}}-\frac{1}{{\theta }_{2}}}$$\end{document}x=klnθ2θ11θ1−1θ2
